# Collect logs of any wood type. The first rung of the first-night chain: no
# tool needed. `#logs` comes from the 26.3 block tag, so birch, spruce and the
# other woods work on any seed. No log in sight means walk to a frontier of the
# perception known map before mining; `explore` sleeps at most 1200 ticks.
#
# Two things were added after run 6 (decision 2026-11-09 "Five next areas"):
#
# An axe from surplus planks. Six planks is deliberate: the axe never competes
# with the first pickaxe (three planks) or the first table (four), it is paid
# from what is left over, and it needs the table already near (two sticks and
# three planks are a 3x3 recipe). `choose_tool` then holds the axe for logs with
# no further code. It sits above the search so a body that stands next to its
# table and holds the planks fixes itself before chopping.
#
# A climb to a log it cannot reach. The mine goes through the child `cut_log`,
# so that a log the crosshair cannot hit (`out_of_reach`: overhead; `no_sight`:
# behind another block) is one failure of the caller's rule. That rule is then
# ineligible for one tick, and the rule under it places a log under the feet
# (`pillar`, one block per call) before the mine tries again. Three failures of
# the mining rule end the skill, which bounds the climb to two blocks. The
# scaffold is a real log but is never a target: `near`, `mine` and `explore`
# all skip it through `praxion_perception::scaffold`. With no
# log to climb on, the skill fails `unreachable` at once. Mining skills other
# than this one do not climb; they stay on the ground.
skill get_wood(n: int = 1)
  doc "Collect at least n logs of any wood type, crafting an axe from surplus planks and climbing a log at a time to a log it cannot reach."
  rules:
    count(#logs) >= $n -> done
    not has(#axes) and count(#planks) >= 6 and count(stick) >= 2 and near(crafting_table, 4) -> craft(wooden_axe)
    not near(#logs, 5) -> explore(#logs)
    near(#logs, 5) -> do cut_log($n)
    count(#logs) >= 1 -> pillar(#logs)
    true -> fail(unreachable)
